<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of the Middle Angles</h1><p class="st-meta">653 CE – 656 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A brief sub-kingdom in the east Midlands created for Penda's son Peada, notable as the place where Christianity first entered Mercian lands.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-angles">Middle Angles</a> were a group of peoples in the east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a>, around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-fens">Fens</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-nene">Nene</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-welland">Welland</a> valleys. Around 653 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia</a> made his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peada">Peada</a> their king. Peada married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhflaed">Alhflaed</a>, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiu">Oswiu of Northumbria</a>, and accepted baptism as a condition of the marriage.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peada">Peada</a> brought four priests, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cedd">Cedd</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diuma">Diuma</a>, who preached among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-angles">Middle Angles</a>, the first Christian mission in Mercian territory. Tradition links Peada to the foundation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterborough-abbey">Medeshamstede</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterborough">Peterborough</a> Abbey. The many small peoples of the region, listed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tribal-hidage">Tribal Hidage</a>, kept their own identities.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda's</a> death in 655 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiu">Oswiu</a> let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peada">Peada</a> rule southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Mercia</a>, but he was murdered in 656, allegedly through his wife's treachery. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-angles">Middle Angles</a> were absorbed into Mercia, though they later had their own bishopric at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-the-middle-angles.jpg" alt="Kingdom of the Middle Angles" width="320" height="392"><figcaption>Image: Amitchell125 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anglo-Saxon_England_2.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>653 CE – 656 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Peada</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Middle Anglia, Middel Engle</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=654&amp;focus=kingdom-of-the-middle-angles" aria-label="Kingdom of the Middle Angles on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4665 311 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of the Middle Angles lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4962 619h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4985 567h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4985 567h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United Kingdom. <a href="/atlas?year=654&amp;focus=kingdom-of-the-middle-angles">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
